 Hello,

so I have a few questions about using the tpm2-tools to generate keys.

1.When running tpm2_createprimary or tpm2_create there will be a rsa
section printed at the end, looking like this:
   ```
     ...
     sym-mode:
       value: (null)
       raw: 0x0
     sym-keybits: 0
     rsa: b75d86bf00004fc66997...<=3D this here
   ```
   I quickly checked it and it looks to be only the public part of the RSA
key, am I right?

2. What does the context do when using tpm2-tools, I assume it is required
because the tpm2 operations are split into
   separate executables so the tools need to keep some shared state using
those context files. Also there is also the
   key handle option in many executables which looks like it can be used
instead of the context for some of the operations.

3. When using `tpm2_create -C primary.ctx -u pub.key -r priv.key` does the
priv.key file now contain the plain-text file.
   My guess is no and it's probably they private key encrypted with some
internal key inside the TPM which is not accessible.
   Is this assumption correct?

4. I still have a bit of trouble understanding the terminology and the
hierarchies,but when I create a new key (using tpm2_create),
   how exactly is this key related to the parent primary key? I guess it is
not derived from it and rather randomly generated but
   just signed/authenticated by the primary key?


Thank you in advance and best regards,

Martin Pietryka

